{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And if a man entices</b> — he speaks to her heart. Thus Onkelos renders: \"אֲרֵי יְשַׁדֵּל\" — שִׁדּוּל in Aramaic is like פִּתּוּי in the holy tongue. <b>He shall surely pay a mohar, he shall make her his wife</b> — he shall fix for her a mohar according to the law of a man for his wife, that he writes her a ketubbah, and he shall marry her. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 22:15). But it is not correct. Rather, the meaning of פִּתּוּי is the turning of desire through falsehood: \"lest your heart be enticed\" (Deuteronomy 11:16), \"and my heart was secretly enticed\" (Job 31:27), \"if my heart was enticed toward a woman\" (Job 31:9). Therefore men whose understanding is not whole enough to discern, so that their heart is led astray by words at the beginning of their affairs, are called פְּתָאִים (the naive), as it says: \"The naive believes everything\" (Proverbs 14:15). And one who seduces a virgin to lie with her turns her desire toward his object through false words and is called מְפַתֶּה.\n\nOnkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 22:15) distinguished the language according to its categories: here he said \"יְשַׁדֵּל,\" which is the language of stratagem and engagement that a person undertakes with another to accomplish his will, whether his effort be in words or in deed. For thus he rendered in \"And a man wrestled with him\" (Genesis 32:25) — \"וְאִשְׁתַּדַּל,\" and Jonathan ben Uzziel translated \"I will turn you back and put hooks in your jaws\" (Ezekiel 38:4) — \"וַאֲשַׁדְּלִנָּךְ,\" and in the Targum of Megillat Ruth, \"Where have you gleaned today and where have you worked?\" (Ruth 2:19) — \"וּלְאָן אִשְׁתַּדַּלְתְּ לְמֶעֱבַד,\" \"The name of the man with whom I worked today is Boaz\" (Ruth 2:19) — \"דְּאִשְׁתַּדַּלִית עִמֵּהּ,\" for the stratagem of a matter that a person schemes to accomplish is called הִשְׁתַּדְּלוּת. And they said in the Mishnah (Avot 2:5): \"In a place where there are no men, strive (הִשְׁתַּדֵּל) to be a man,\" and in the Gemara (Berakhot 58a): \"A person should always strive (יִשְׁתַּדֵּל) to go out to meet the kings of Israel,\" and in Scripture (Daniel 6:15): \"הֲוָה מִשְׁתַּדַּר לְהַצָּלוּתֵהּ\" — striving and making stratagems. And in my opinion, from this language comes \"וּמְרַד וְאֶשְׁתַּדּוּר מִתְעֲבֶד בַּהּ\" (Ezra 4:19) — rebellion and great stratagem, for these letters are interchanged even in the holy tongue: \"מַזָּלוֹת\" (2 Kings 23:5) and \"מַזָּרוֹת\" (Job 38:32), \"נִמְלְצוּ\" (Psalms 119:103) and \"נִמְרְצוּ\" (Job 6:25), \"מִפְלְשֵׂי עָב\" (Job 37:39) — \"מִפְרְשֵׂי עָב\" (Job 36:29), and likewise in Aramaic: \"וַאֲלוּ\" (Daniel 7:8) — \"וַאֲרוּ\" (Daniel 7:7). Of \"שַׁרְשֶׁרֶת\" (Exodus 28:14) the Sages said (Mikva'ot 10:5) — שַׁלְשֶׁלֶת. And there are manuscripts in which the Mishnah (Avot 2:5) reads הִשְׁתַּדֵּר לִהְיוֹת אִישׁ. Therefore Onkelos rendered \"כִּי יְפַתֶּה\" — \"אֲרֵי יְשַׁדֵּל\": he manoeuvres and coaxes the virgin through many devices until he lies with her. And because the enticement of virgins occurs through many devices — sometimes with words, sometimes with money, sometimes with falsehood that he leads her astray, sometimes in truth that his desire is to marry her — he did not assign a single distinct term but called it הִשְׁתַּדְּלוּת. But in \"lest your heart be enticed\" (Deuteronomy 11:16) he rendered \"דִּלְמָא יִטְעֶה\" — lest it be led astray.\n\nAs for what the Rav explained regarding \"מָהֹר יִמְהָרֶנָּה לּוֹ\" — that he shall fix for her a mohar according to the law of a man for his wife who writes her a ketubbah (Mekhilta ad loc.) — it is not true, for the seducer, if he marries her, pays no fine, and if he divorces her after marriage, she has no claim against him by Torah law, since the ketubbah is of rabbinic origin. Rather, the meaning of מֹהַר is the gifts that a person sends to his betrothed — silver vessels, gold vessels, and garments — for the needs of the wedding canopy and the marriage; these are what are called סִבְלוֹנוֹת in the language of the Sages. Thus they said: מֻהְרֵי הָדְרֵי (Bava Batra 145a). And Onkelos rendered \"Increase for me exceedingly the mohar and the gift\" (Genesis 34:12) — \"אַסְגּוֹ עָלַי לַחֲדָא מֻהְרִין וּמַתְּנָן,\" and he was not vowing to write for her many ketubbot exceedingly, but rather the mohar is סִבְלוֹנוֹת as I have explained. And it is possible that this word is derived from \"מְהֵרָה חוּשָׁה\" (1 Samuel 20:38), because it is the first thing hurried in marriage, for the groom hurries and sends ahead that gift, and afterward he comes to his father-in-law's house to conduct the marriage or the celebration, as the Sages mentioned the meal of the סִבְלוֹנוֹת (Pesahim 49a).\n\nThe meaning of \"מָהֹר יִמְהָרֶנָּה לּוֹ לְאִשָּׁה\" — that he shall send her סִבְלוֹנוֹת and wedding needs, to make her his wife — hints that they can delay matters. And Scripture said it in this language and did not say that he shall take her for himself, because there is no commandment upon him to marry her unless he wishes; and if he does not desire her in the first place, he pays. And afterward Scripture says (Exodus 22:16) that if her father refuses to give her to him, he shall weigh out money according to the mohar that men give to virgins married to them — and the reason is that he has disqualified her in the eyes of the young men, so that her father will need to increase her gift, and they will not give her a mohar, and it is fitting that the seducer pay it. And our Sages said (Ketubbot 10a) that the amount of this mohar is fixed according to the law of the violated woman (Deuteronomy 22:29), for it is fifty silver, and the law of the violator and the seducer are equal in this matter. But Scripture distinguished between the violator and the seducer, for there it is written \"and she shall be his wife; he cannot send her away all his days,\" and the reason is that it is the way of the world for desirable young men to seduce virgins — the beautiful daughters of the great — and it is not fitting that a sinner should profit. Therefore it explained that he may not marry her against their will but must pay them. And because she too sinned in the matter, it did not impose upon him the punishment that he must marry her against his will; it suffices that he pay the fine. And once he marries her with her consent and her father's consent, she is with him like all other women — she has no ketubbah claim against him by Torah law. Likewise it is the way of the sons of the great to violate the daughters of those of lower standing who have no power against them; therefore it said of the violator \"and she shall be his wife\" — against his will.\n\nAccording to the opinion of our Sages (Ketubbot 39b), there too, both she and her father can refuse, for it is not fitting that he take her against their will, and he would do two evils to her, and sometimes she may be repulsed by him, and it is not possible that she be degraded through his sin; and the right judgment is that marriage should be in her hand and not in his — that he marry her against his will — so that the daughters of Israel not be ownerless property for men of might. Now this law applies only to a נַעֲרָה, as in the law of the violator. But there Scripture specified \"When a man finds a young woman, a virgin\" (Deuteronomy 22:28), whereas here it did not mention נַעֲרָה, and the reason is that the נַעֲרָה mentioned there serves only to exclude the בּוֹגֶרֶת, who is an independent woman, whereas the minor is also included (Ketubbot 40a). Here there was no need to exclude the בּוֹגֶרֶת, for it is obvious that the seducer of a בּוֹגֶרֶת pays nothing, for he acted with her consent, and the father has no right in his daughter after the days of youth, as it says (Numbers 30:17) \"in her youth, in her father's house.\" And it says here <b>if her father utterly refuses to give her to him</b>, to hint that it is he who gives her to a husband, for he holds the right to the money of her betrothal when she is a minor and a נַעֲרָה, as they expounded: all the advantages of youth belong to her father (Kiddushin 3a). But in the case of the violator, Scripture needed to write נַעֲרָה to exclude the בּוֹגֶרֶת, for it would have occurred to us that he should pay her, but the decree of Scripture is otherwise — and the reason is that when she stands on her own awareness she can guard herself from violation.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 22:15) that מֹהַר is a connected noun-form, and likewise \"they multiplied their pains; they hastened\" (Psalms 16:4). This is not correct, but rather it is as I have explained from the words of our Sages, of blessed memory. And in my opinion, \"אַחֵר מָהֲרוּ\" means that they were hurried of heart toward another god, going after him in their haste without reflection and without understanding. And according to the grammarians (Radak in Sefer ha-Shorashim, root אחר) who explain that they gave a mohar to another god — the meaning is that they offered him sacrifice and meal-offering."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The Ramban structures this as one continuous comment on the verse. I have preserved the paragraph breaks implied by the source's internal line-break tags (<br>) as paragraph divisions in the translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:19:05.047867055Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}